WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.860
STEREN GIANNINI: Hi,
I'm Steren Giannini.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.180
Welcome to Cloud Run--

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.190
What's New?

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.470
So a year ago, we launched,
publicly, Cloud Run.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.370
In this session,
I'm going to go--

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.620
I'm going to go over
the few improvements

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.140
that we've released since then.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.840
This session will be
structured in three dimensions.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.550
We'll see how Cloud Run is now
even more enterprise-ready,

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.980
how it is more
developer-friendly,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.980
and the how it gives
you more flexibility.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.860
But first, I want to recap.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.540
What is Cloud Run?

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.760
So Cloud Run allows
you to run containers

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.010
on a fully managed environment.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.590
We can call that serverless.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.430
Its main characteristics
are that it auto-scales,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.110
there is no
infrastructure to manage,

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.910
and it allows you to go fast.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.790
It makes you productive.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.720
So as I said, you can deploy
containers in seconds.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.480
And thanks to that, you can
use any language, any library

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.470
you want because of containers.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.180
We spent a lot of
time delivering

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.610
an enhanced developer experience
so that we make you productive

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.758
when using Cloud Run.

00:01:20.758 --> 00:01:23.050
A few nice things is that
when you deploy to Cloud Run,

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.230
you get, out-of-the-box,
an automatic HTTPS endpoint

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.030
for your service.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.740
And you can attach your own
custom domain very easily.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.540
Cloud Run is portable--

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.100
first, because it accepts the
container standard as an input,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.250
but also because it
is based on Knative,

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.980
an open source project that
allows you to run serverless

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.790
workloads anywhere.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.880
And Cloud Run is also
available for Anthos,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.520
allowing you to run
the same Cloud Run

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.170
workloads in your own cluster,
be it on Google Cloud,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.360
on other clouds, or on prem.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.690
And finally, Cloud
Run is pay per use.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.900
Your literally only pay
for when your containers

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.860
are processing requests
rounded up to 100 milliseconds.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.690
So with that, let's dive in.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:18.210
Let's see how Cloud Run is now
even more enterprise-ready.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:23.130
So first, when we launched a
year ago, we launched to beta.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:26.700
And today we are
generally available.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.490
Of course, that comes
with all of the goodness

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:33.880
of a generally available product
of GCP in terms of support

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.850
and in terms of
service-level agreement.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:42.040
Cloud Run has 99.95%
availability SLA.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.320
Then we have been
very hard at work

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.100
to turn on Cloud Run in
many different regions

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.580
all over the world.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.000
So what you see on that
map are, the green dots

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.970
are where Cloud Run
is available today.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.130
And the blue dots
is where we plan

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.800
to operate Cloud Run
by the end of the year.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.070
So as you've seen, we
now cover all continents.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.780
And we are hard at work turning
up even more regions very soon.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.910
Then, one of the
key features that we

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.330
added that was
requested by many of you

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.250
was the ability to access your
resources that are in your VPC,

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.590
in your Virtual Private Cloud.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.060
So for example, thanks to
serverless VPC connectors,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.340
Cloud Run can now connect
to Cloud Memorystore Redis,

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.690
or Memcache, or simply connect
to a private IP of a Compute

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.000
Engine VM, for example.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.100
Next, we-- you know, when we
raised Cloud Run, you could--

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.340
anytime you were deploying a
new revision of a Cloud Run

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.520
service, this revision was
starting to serve traffic

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.370
as fast as possible to 100%.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.290
Well, we all know
that's not really

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.020
how we should deliver
software, right?

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.590
So what we added
is the capability

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.180
to control the rollout
of few revisions,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.580
or even to roll back
to a previous revision.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.450
So let's see an example of
what we call a blue/green

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:08.450
deployment.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.130
So let's assume we
are in a revision.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.520
That will be called blue.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.000
And we want to deploy to
something called green.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.050
So the first step
is to, of course,

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.709
deploy that new revision.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:23.570
So let's assume I want to deploy
a new image of my container.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.300
So I would use a regular
command you would know,

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.850
except that this time, I'm going
to say, do not serve traffic.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.420
And I'm going to give it a tag--

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.345
green for this example.

00:04:34.345 --> 00:04:36.720
Of course, I'm showing you
this through the command line,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.220
but you can shooting exactly
the same from the Cloud Console.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.070
Once I've done that, this
revision that was created

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.000
is not going to serve traffic.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.100
But I, as a developer, or
my team, can give it a try,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.870
can invoke it under a very
specific URL that contains

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.550
the tag that we just added.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.350
So once I've validated that this
new revision is ready to go,

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:04.040
is behaving correctly, then
it's time to send traffic

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.960
to it gradually.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.140
So I will start--

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.060
I will start small.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.390
I will start by sending
1% of the traffic

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:12.950
to that new revision.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.590
So in that case, I'm using
the update traffic command.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:20.990
And I'm going to say,
send 1% to the tag green.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.080
And the system will know that
the tag green is attached

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.000
to a specific
revision and that 1%

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.040
of the incoming external traffic
should go to that new revision.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.900
From my end, what
I should do now

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.420
is take a look at my
monitoring dashboard, my logs,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.960
filter by this revision
name, and double-check

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.830
that everything's fine.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:45.170
Once I confirm that everything
is good to continue,

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:48.080
then it's time to send more
traffic to that revision.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.280
So I'm going to send
10%, wait a little bit,

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.450
double-check everything is fine,
send 50%, wait a little bit,

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.270
check everything is
fine, and finally, send

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:03.050
100% of the incoming traffic
to that new revision.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.390
And by the way, at
Google, we mostly

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:08.090
do all virtual rollouts
over four days,

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:12.870
making sure that everything
is healthy between each step.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.570
Once I've done that,
I'm good to go.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.970
This new revision is serving
all of the incoming traffic.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:22.260
And I consider this just
a new current revision.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.960
If at any point in time
during the rollout,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.680
or at any point in
time, I can roll back

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.570
to a previous revision.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.300
To do so, it's one click
in the user interface

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.720
or one command with GCloud,
which is an update--

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.220
I update the traffic,
but this time,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.050
to a specific revision name.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.700
And I send 100% of
the traffic to it,

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.940
which means I'm going to
roll back to that revision.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.720
So hopefully, that can help
you mostly safely release

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.690
your container and your software
by giving you more control

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.810
and allowing you to traffic
split between revisions.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:04.750
All right, then we added support
for Google Cloud Artifact

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:05.810
Registry.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:09.400
So Artifact Registry is the
evolution of Google Cloud

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.110
Container Registry.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.330
And the benefits
of it is that you

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:17.860
can have content or registries
in very specific regions

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.030
in the regions you want.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:21.860
So that's even better
for data localities

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.030
so that you can store your
containers wherever you want.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:28.840
You can even use your
own encryption keys

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.640
to encrypt those
containers in the registry.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.510
Cloud Artifact Registry
has a free tier,

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.920
which is always good.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.080
And you can have per repository
access control using Cloud IAM.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.420
So in the example on the
right, what I'm doing

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:49.300
is that I'm deploying to Cloud
Run from Artifact Registry

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:52.090
where my registry is actually
from Europe north 1, a very

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.050
specific region in Europe.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.130
OK, next, in the
enterprise-ready category,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.790
we added support for Google
Cloud load balancing.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.820
So what that helps
you do, for example,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:12.230
is that if you deploy the same
Cloud Run service in three

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:16.220
different regions, you
can add, on top of them,

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.650
Google Cloud load
balancer and expose

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:22.010
a global endpoint that
will route requests

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:27.650
to the closest region in
order to reduce latency,

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:31.460
but also to be resilient
to original outages in case

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.230
something goes bad in
a Google Cloud region.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.110
So let's see an example.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.030
Here, somebody
from San Francisco

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.190
connects to my global endpoint.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.010
Well, Google Cloud load
balancer will run that request

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.040
to my cloud and service,
which is in the US.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.470
But now if somebody
from Paris connects

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:51.890
to that same global endpoint,
then Google Cloud load balancer

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:55.040
will send the request
to Europe west 1.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.180
So you see how you can build
global services with increased

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.380
availability and performances
on top of multiple Cloud Run

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.020
services.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:06.960
But that's not all.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.720
Google Cloud load balancer
allows you to use Cloud CDN.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.400
So now, by using Google Cloud
load balancer and Cloud CDN

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.870
on top of Cloud Run,
you can, for example,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.830
reduce the load on your
Cloud Run service or increase

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.050
performance of your
official service.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.880
So let's see this example.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.330
The very first request comes in.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.760
This one is not cached, because
nobody has seen it before.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.120
This one will reach
the Cloud Run service.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:38.050
Cloud Run can respond with a
special response header, which

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:40.510
is cache-control,
and saying, I want

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:44.480
you to cache this
request for 300 seconds.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:44.980
Good.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.980
Next time a client
asks the same,

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:50.740
Cloud CDN will be able
to return the request

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.620
without even it needing to
reach your Cloud Run service.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:58.120
So you see how by
caching some request,

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.550
you can drastically reduce
the load on your service

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.510
and performance.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:03.695
So that's not all.

00:10:03.695 --> 00:10:07.600
Google Cloud load balancer
integration gives us even more.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.730
Identity aware
proxy is something

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.790
that allows you
to add, basically,

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:16.570
a login screen on top of
your Cloud Run service.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:19.870
And this login screen can
enable you to grant access

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:23.420
to only certain users
inside your organization,

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.830
for example, your employee,
for an internal application--

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:31.350
so once again, identity
aware proxy via Google

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:35.270
Cloud load balancing
on top of Cloud Run.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:38.850
And very similarly, you
can use Cloud Armor thanks

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.440
to Google Cloud load balancing.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:44.340
So Cloud Armor is a web
application firewall.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:51.240
It will help you in case of
a denial of service attack.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:57.480
And it will help you accept only
some IPs from certain IP ranges

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:58.840
or geographies.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.930
So in this example on
the right, I am only

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:05.940
accepting IPs from a range,
and the others get rejected.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.510
So the rejected ones don't even
reach my Cloud Run service.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:13.510
This is all handled by Cloud
Armor via Google Cloud load

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:16.250
balancer.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:19.070
So hopefully we
are convincing you

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.410
how Cloud Run is,
even more than before,

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:25.940
enterprise-ready with all of
those very important features

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.520
that you have been
asking us to deliver.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:34.430
Now let's see how Cloud Run is
even more developer-friendly.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.750
So first, we've
heard your feedback.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.180
And thank you for sharing
that you like Cloud Run.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:45.800
We measured, via some
Cloud Console surveys,

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:50.060
that most of you are very
satisfied with Cloud Run.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.350
And so we are
pleased to hear that.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.970
And this is one of the highest
satisfaction score that we've

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.010
seen for Google Cloud.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.660
So we are very proud of this.

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:02.150
But we haven't stopped there.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:07.400
We are keeping delivering many
developer-focused features

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.350
to make you even
more productive.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:14.120
And one of them is the ability
to deploy from a YAML fine.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.820
So if you want to store the
configuration of your Cloud Run

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:23.500
services in your version
control system in config files,

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:24.655
well, you can.

00:12:24.655 --> 00:12:27.820
And actually, because Cloud Run
implements the Knative serving

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.360
API, this file is
exactly a Knative

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.410
serving resource descriptor.

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:35.690
So this is what--

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.680
with that, you can achieve
what some people called GitOps,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:42.470
which is, you use Git
as your source of truth

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:45.690
when it comes to your
resource configuration.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.930
So we've added a new
command to G Cloud called

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.750
gcloud run services replace.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:56.450
And that takes a YAML
file of your service.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.910
Next, we've worked with
the Cloud Code team

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:03.110
to bring Cloud Run integration
inside the Cloud Code IDE

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:07.530
plugins for IntelliJ
and Visual Studio Code.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.090
So this plugin will help
you bootstrap new Clout Run

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.920
apps with sample apps.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.310
It will help you manage your
services and your revisions,

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:17.900
see their properties.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:21.260
It will help you build your
source code into containers

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:23.420
and deploy them to Cloud Run.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:27.380
And my favorite, it will help
you run your Cloud Run services

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.650
locally inside a
containerized environment that

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:36.550
looks a lot what you would have
if you deployed to Cloud Run.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:41.790
Next, Google Cloud has released
Google Cloud buildpacks.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.250
So buildpacks are
basically recipes

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.640
to create containers in very--

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.030
in a set of very
popular languages.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:52.770
In our case, the Google Cloud
buildpacks support Go, Node.js,

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:55.530
Python, Java, and .NET.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.210
Thanks to buildpacks,
you don't need

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.690
to worry about writing
Dockerfiles anymore.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.660
If you see the
example on the right,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.360
my source code doesn't
contain any Dockerfile.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.810
And I'm able to build
it with Cloud Build,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:14.520
using the --pack command, into
a container that I can then

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:17.460
deploy to Cloud Run.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.860
Buildpacks are-- those
buildpacks are open source.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:25.300
And buildpacks are
a CNCF open standard

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:27.530
that are used by many vendors.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.070
So we are very proud
to have them today

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:34.170
to help you build
the containers.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.820
Next is that when you
deploy to Cloud Run,

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:39.700
and when you build your
container from a Git

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:44.680
repository, we now capture
the context of this container

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.020
and display that right
inside the user interface.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:50.650
So if you see on
that slide, you see

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:54.340
how, in addition to the
container image URL,

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:58.240
we give you a link to
the build that was used

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.240
to create that container
as well as a link

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:06.550
to the GitHub repository at
this specific commit for you

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:08.260
to troubleshoot
in case something

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:12.130
is wrong with that
particular container.

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:15.460
And in a similar
fashion, we are also

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.370
helping you embrace
the best practices

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:20.290
of continuous deployment.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:22.840
So right now, from the
Cloud Run user interface,

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:26.450
you can, in a few
clicks, configure

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.210
a continuous deployment pipeline
for your Cloud Run service.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:31.520
So let's take a look.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.903
Right now I'm creating
a new service.

00:15:33.903 --> 00:15:36.320
And I'm going to-- instead of
picking a specific container

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.580
that I already built, I'm going
to select a Git repository.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:44.150
So when I click this, it asks
me to authenticate with GitHub

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:48.290
if GitHub is where your
Git repositories are.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:51.710
Then I can select one
of my repositories.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:54.320
I validate the
build configuration,

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:58.690
giving it a location of my
Dockerfile and my branch.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:15:59.630
I hit Save.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:03.260
And then Cloud Run will
create the Cloud Run service

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:07.850
as well as create a Cloud Build
trigger that will automatically

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:10.250
watch this repository
for new commits,

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:12.560
and when they are pushed
to this repository,

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:15.830
will automatically build
and deploy to Cloud Run.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:18.020
So in that case, we
trigger the first build.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:22.300
And your service is now
serving with the code that

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:23.960
was in your Git repository.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:27.620
Over time, we even
show you the history

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:29.900
of the builds right
inside the Cloud Run user

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:33.260
interface for convenience.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:37.370
But that's not all what we've
delivered for developers.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:40.190
I'm very excited
to say that we are

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:44.120
launching new ways to
trigger your Cloud Run

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:47.000
services via events.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.550
So basically, you can now
trigger Cloud Run services

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:54.590
when events are emitted from
other Google Cloud resources.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:58.140
So this is all based
on Cloud audit logs.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:02.240
So you might know that GCP
resources, anytime they are

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:05.990
changed, they emit audit logs.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:07.994
So very easily,
what you can do now

00:17:07.994 --> 00:17:09.369
is you can trigger
your Cloud Run

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:15.540
service any time one of your
GCP resources is mutated.

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:19.740
And the events that you receive
to your Cloud Run service

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.849
are delivered in an
open standard again,

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:26.265
the cloud events open standard,
which was a CNCF project.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:32.040
So I'm also very
excited to say that we

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.410
are launching Cloud workflows.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:41.110
So Cloud workflows allow you
to orchestrate your serverless

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:47.320
task with the rich and
declarative programming model.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.130
For example, you want to process
events with multiple Cloud Run

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.960
services, you can
chain API calls.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:56.410
You can automate
infrastructure management.

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:59.920
You can implement retry
polices very easily

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:01.930
thanks to Cloud workflows.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:05.320
So in this example on the right,
what I'm doing, very simply,

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:08.600
is, every day, I want to stop
all of my developer machines.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.600
So to do that, every
day at 6:00 PM,

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:16.120
one Cloud Run service returns
me a list of all of the dev VMs.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:19.540
Another one extracts
their statuses.

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:21.670
And for each VM, I
extract the status.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:23.650
If it is running, I stop it.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:27.460
And then I email
the owner of this VM

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:30.850
to make sure that tomorrow,
they don't forget to stop them.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:34.120
So you see how you can
build complex workflows

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:37.225
by bringing together very
simple serverless pieces.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:41.180
So that's it.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:44.660
That's how we have made
Cloud Run even more

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:47.660
developer-friendly
over the last month.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.440
And we are not stopping
there, of course.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.612
Now let's see how we've--

00:18:52.612 --> 00:18:54.070
we've heard your feedback.

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:56.860
And we've been removing
some of the limits

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:58.600
that Cloud Run had
at the beginning

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.020
by giving you more flexibility.

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:03.940
So first, we heard you.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.140
You want more RAM.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.670
You want more CPUs.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:09.250
So you can now go
up to 4 gigabytes

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:10.970
of memory and four vCPUs.

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:16.960
On a very similar
fashion, in the past,

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:20.860
you were limited to 15 minutes
of request processing time.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:23.810
We are now extending
that to an hour.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:28.000
So you can process things
up to one hour on Cloud Run.

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:34.840
Next, once again, we
are delivering something

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:38.530
that a lot of you
have been asking for.

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:43.630
Today, as I mentioned, Cloud
Run auto-scales, and notably,

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:45.730
scales down to 0.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.350
So that's great.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.990
But that means that when
there is no traffic,

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.520
and suddenly there is
traffic that comes in,

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.260
you have what we call a cold
start, which is the time

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.880
it takes to start--

00:19:57.880 --> 00:19:59.710
to go from zero
container instances

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:02.290
to one continuing
instance or more.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:06.100
And this usually depends a
lot on what your container is

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:09.230
doing at startup time.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:11.870
With many instances,
you can say, hey,

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:15.580
I want a minimum of, for
example, two instances

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:18.380
always on at any point in time.

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:19.990
And when that's
the case, Cloud Run

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:23.080
will make sure that
your number of instances

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:28.360
never goes below this specific--
the number you specify.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.430
So that's great for
improving performance,

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:34.870
notably when it
scales down to zero.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:37.750
But the good news is that
those instances, when

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.150
they are not in
use, actually are

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.500
cheaper than active instances.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:47.970
Another thing we've added
to help you better handle

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:51.570
the auto-scaling of Cloud
Run is that we are now

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:58.750
sending a signal before the
instance is shutting down.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.360
So that gives you the
time, for example,

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:04.010
to close any open
database connection

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:06.540
or to send any data
that you might not

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:09.090
have sent in kept in memory.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.250
By sending this
SIGTERM signal, you

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.890
have up to 10 seconds
of processing time

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:16.200
where you can do something
before the instance is

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.540
shut down.

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:20.270
We've also heard you.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.790
We added support for gRPC.

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:27.350
So now, out of the box,
your Cloud Run service

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:29.325
can expose a gRPC server.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:34.790
Nothing to configure,
just your code

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.190
has to start a gRPC
server that listens

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:40.250
on the same port as the one
we are sending regular HTTP

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:41.840
requests.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.450
So gRPC on a service
infrastructure-- yes,

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:48.540
that's possible with Cloud Run.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:51.990
And lastly, we've also
heard your feedback.

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:55.630
We added support for
server-side streaming.

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:59.760
So what that means is that you
can now stream your responses

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:01.140
back to the clients.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.570
You are not limited
anymore by the response

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:05.710
size of 32 megabytes.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.670
And the responses are
not buffered anymore.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:13.250
That allows you to stream
HTTP and gRPC responses.

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:15.860
In the example on the
right, what I'm doing is

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:19.190
my small web server is
actually serving a video file

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:20.695
into the client.

00:22:20.695 --> 00:22:22.070
And I'm using this
special header

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.320
transfer encoding chunked.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:28.580
That means that the client
will be downloading the stream

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.470
of the bytes of this video.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.160
So hopefully this
has been moving

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:38.070
some of the limits that were
initially part of Cloud Run.

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:40.380
And don't worry, we
are not stopping here.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:45.370
We are keeping removing
those existing limits.

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:49.500
So hopefully, with this
talk, I convinced you

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:54.280
how Cloud Run is now, even more
than ever, enterprise-ready,

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:57.370
and even more than ever,
developer-friendly,

00:22:57.370 --> 00:23:00.580
and by removing some
of its original limits,

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:02.630
gives you more flexibility.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:04.780
We have been very hard
at work to deliver all

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:08.750
of these features last year.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.570
And we are, of course,
not going to stop here.

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:14.770
I want to thank you for
attending this session

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:17.470
and we can't wait to
see the great thing is

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:20.160
you will build with Cloud Run.

